EBS/Elastic Block Store volume= network drive attached to instances
only mounted to one instance at a time you can also have a multi-attach feature.
bound to a specific availability/AZ zone
has a provisioned capacity and delays because it goes over the network
delete on termination attribute:
  1) controls EBS behavior when EC2 is terminated
    1.1) By default, the root EBS is deleted
    1.2) other volumes of EBS are not deleted
  2) controled by AWS console/AWS CLI

EBS snapshots:
  1) makes a backup of EBS volume 
  2) copy snapshots across AZ or Region
EBS snapshots feature:
  1) EBS snapshot archive = 75% cheaper but takes 24 to 72 hours for restoring
  2) recycle bin for EBS: setup rules to retain deleted snapshots, specify retention
  3) fast snapshot restore/FSR: full initization of snapshot and cost very much 

AMI/Amazon Machine Image= 
customization of EC2 by adding your own software,configuration,OS,montoring tools,.... 
Makes it faster to boot and can be copied across regions.
3 kinds of AMI:
  1) public AMI: provided by AWS
  2) Own AMI
  3) AWS marketplace AMI
Processes for AMI's:
  1) start
  2) stop
  3) build
  4) launch
EC2 instance store:
  1) used for high-performance hardware disk
  2) better I/O performance
  3) if they're stopped you lose their storage/ephemeral
  4) good for buffer/cache/scratch data/ temp cont
  5) risk of data loss = hardware fails
EBS volumes 6 types:
  1) gp2/gp3: general purpose ssd volume balances price and performance
    1.1) gp2: linked I/O and throughput
    1.2) gp3: indepently can set I/O and throughput
  2) io 1/io2 block exprecess: high performance ssd volume 
  3) st 1: low cost hdd volume designed for frequently accessed, throughput intensive workloads
  4) sc 1: lowest cost hdd volume less frequently accessed workloads
only gp2/gp3 and io 1/io 2 block express used as boot volumes. And volumes are chartarized by size,throughput,IOPS (I/O ops per sec)
